\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The optimal policy across kernels and parameters. (a)~For the temporary-plus-resilient kernel ($\eta =0.5$, $\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$), the two moving-average rates $b_1,b_2$ against risk aversion: the slow rate $b_1\to 0$ as $\lambda \to 0$ (the position becomes non-stationary without risk) and both grow with $\lambda $. (b)~The position impulse response $g_x(\tau )$ by kernel ($\theta =1$, normalized at $\tau =0.3$): the exponential and two-average kernels have finite memory (exponential cutoff), the power-law kernel a heavy tail (Section~\ref {sec:fractional}), and the Markowitz limit is an instantaneous spike. (c)~The power-law policy across $\beta $: a scale-free kernel of slope $(\beta -1)/2$.}}{12}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:structure}{{2}{12}{The optimal policy across kernels and parameters. (a)~For the temporary-plus-resilient kernel ($\eta =0.5$, $\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$), the two moving-average rates $b_1,b_2$ against risk aversion: the slow rate $b_1\to 0$ as $\lambda \to 0$ (the position becomes non-stationary without risk) and both grow with $\lambda $. (b)~The position impulse response $g_x(\tau )$ by kernel ($\theta =1$, normalized at $\tau =0.3$): the exponential and two-average kernels have finite memory (exponential cutoff), the power-law kernel a heavy tail (Section~\ref {sec:fractional}), and the Markowitz limit is an instantaneous spike. (c)~The power-law policy across $\beta $: a scale-free kernel of slope $(\beta -1)/2$}{figure.2}{}}
\newlabel{eq:rate-policy}{{21}{12}{The power-law kernel}{equation.21}{}}
