\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Anatomy of the trading filter, exponential versus power-law transient impact ($\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$, $\beta =0.5$; small $\lambda =0.1$, $\eta =0.05$). (a)~Transfer function $|H(\omega )|=1/\sqrt  {\hat  n(\omega )}$: both share the low-frequency Markowitz plateau $1/\sqrt  \lambda $ (edge $\omega _c$) and roll off as $\omega ^{-1}$ under the temporary cost at high frequency (edges $\omega _e$ for the exponential, $\omega _*$ for the power law); between them the finite-memory exponential settles on the plateau $1/\sqrt  {2\kappa \gamma }$ while the long-memory power law slides as $\omega ^{-(1+\beta )/2}$. (b)~Impulse response $h(\tau )=\mathcal  {F}^{-1}[\hat  n_+^{-1}]$: the temporary cost caps the short lag at $1/\sqrt  \eta $; past it the exponential filter cuts off (short memory) and the power law leaves an algebraic tail (long memory).}}{14}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:transfer}{{3}{14}{Anatomy of the trading filter, exponential versus power-law transient impact ($\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$, $\beta =0.5$; small $\lambda =0.1$, $\eta =0.05$). (a)~Transfer function $|H(\omega )|=1/\sqrt {\hat n(\omega )}$: both share the low-frequency Markowitz plateau $1/\sqrt \lambda $ (edge $\omega _c$) and roll off as $\omega ^{-1}$ under the temporary cost at high frequency (edges $\omega _e$ for the exponential, $\omega _*$ for the power law); between them the finite-memory exponential settles on the plateau $1/\sqrt {2\kappa \gamma }$ while the long-memory power law slides as $\omega ^{-(1+\beta )/2}$. (b)~Impulse response $h(\tau )=\mathcal {F}^{-1}[\hat n_+^{-1}]$: the temporary cost caps the short lag at $1/\sqrt \eta $; past it the exponential filter cuts off (short memory) and the power law leaves an algebraic tail (long memory)}{figure.3}{}}
